            "embedding_text": "Authority: Account_Takeover\n\n        Document: 2506.10842v1\n\n        Chapter:\n        \n        \n\n        Section:\n        \n\n        Heading:\n        \n\n        Content:\n\n        Fraud in financial systems manifests through patterns such as **nocturnal vulnerability** (increased transaction risks during nighttime), **rapid successive uses** (frequent, high-volume transactions), **spending sprees** (abnormal transaction amounts), and **outlier detection** (unusual behavior in transactional data). These patterns are exacerbated by **time-based anomalies** and **network-based risks**, where interconnected transactions (e.g., in cryptocurrency or mobile money ecosystems) reveal illicit activities. Advanced systems like **Stripe Radar** and **Mastercard Decision Intelligence** leverage these patterns to detect fraud, while frameworks like **Adaptive Risk-Scoring (ARF)** balance behavioral anomalies with transactional context."
